    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## What I changed\n\nI added a validation step to the `POST /api/owners` OfficeFloor pipeline that rejects a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400.\n\n- **`ValidateRegistrationDate.java`** \u2014 new function step. Reads the request body via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`; when a `registrationDate` is supplied and is `isAfter(LocalDate.now())`, it throws the checked `FutureRegistrationDateException`. An omitted date is left alone (the server still defaults it in `ResolveRegistrationDate`).\n- **`FutureRegistrationDateException.java`** \u2014 new checked escalation carrying the offending date and server date.\n- **`FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `400 Bad Request` with a `ProblemDetail` body, matching the existing `InvalidPostcode` handler pattern.\n- **`...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml`** \u2014 escalation wiring to the handler.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted `validateRegistrationDate` right after `validatePostcode` (in the validation phase, before the date is resolved/defaulted), so a future date is a 400 rather than being masked by a later step.\n\nThe check is placed before `resolveRegistrationDate` deliberately \u2014 it validates the *raw supplied* date, and only when one was actually supplied.",
